+# SRW-1893.1 / P45 — the ownership ledger under the fill reader
+
+Ticket: shyamsivakumar/strategy-replay-workbench#2180
+Source: #1893 (Parity P45), re-cut of #2175 at `7cf4e72abfc3c227f013b4be6d4e589f4a769be3`
+Slice: 1 of 4 — the ownership module only
+
+## Proof
+
+```
+node --experimental-sqlite scripts/srw1893_1_ownership_fill_proof.cjs
+```
+
+155 assertions, all passing, against a credential-free ledger: no executor, no
+adapter, no broker, no network. The machine receipt is
+`evidence/SRW-1893/ownership-fill-receipt.json`, written by the proof itself and
+carrying the frozen input hash, the frozen expected hash, the check count, the
+exact head it ran against (`head`) and the hash of the runtime file it ran
+against (`codeSha`). The proof reads the head from `git rev-parse HEAD`, so the
+receipt always names the commit whose tree the assertions ran on, and `codeSha`
+lets a verifier at the final head confirm the code is the code that ran.
+
+## Red signature on the predecessor base
+
+The proof script does not exist on the base, so the first red signature is
+`Cannot find module`. With the script present and the ownership change reverted,
+the proof fails on behaviour, not on a missing function name:
+
+```
+AssertionError [ERR_ASSERTION]: a null fill is accepted as ours with an unknown quantity
+true !== 'unknown'
+```
+
+## What each acceptance criterion pins
+
+| Criterion | Assertion |
+| --- | --- |
+| 1 | a null, blank or absent fill returns `'unknown'`, leaves the stored fill, price, time and status untouched, and is not an identity conflict |
+| 1 | `true`, `false`, `[]`, `'1e0'`, `'1.5'`, `'-1'` and an out-of-range value are identity conflicts and credit no fill, price, time or status |
+| 1 | `fillNumber` refuses every value `Number()` would coerce, and accepts a number or a digit string |
+| 2 | a broker order id already bound to another record is refused before any write, and the refused observation credits nothing |
+| 3 | a lower fill and an unreadable fill each set `replacementPending` on a fresh record without lowering the stored fill or overwriting the settlement status |
+| 3 | an equal-quantity and an increasing observation each record the hazard and credit the fill |
+| 3 | a later observation that no longer reports the hazard clears the flag and restores the broker's own status |
+| 4 | on an equal quantity the group fill names the observation nearer the child, read from the root and from the child |
+| 4 | an agreeing observation supplies a missing execution time, and two absent prices count as agreeing |
+| 4 | a disagreeing price never donates its execution time |
+| 5 | an out-of-range time is dropped, never stored, and never blocks a readable fill |
+| 5 | the last representable date is a time, one millisecond past it is not, and the first readable key wins |
+| 5 | an array, an object or a boolean time is never dated |
+
+## Guards, each verified non-vacuous
+
+Every guard below was checked by reverting the named fix in the working tree,
+watching the matching assertion fail, and restoring it. Fourteen mutations, all
+fourteen caught.
+
+- `fillNumber` — the strict parser refuses booleans, arrays, objects, exponent
+  strings, fractions, negatives and blanks.
+- `brokerOrderIdFree` — one broker order id answers for one record only.
+- `hazard` — the replacement hazard is its own flag and survives a lower or
+  unreadable fill.
+- `nearestChild` — the group fill names the observation nearer the child, for
+  priced and unpriced ties, from either end of the chain.
+- `agreeingTime` — an agreeing observation supplies a missing time; two absent
+  prices agree.
+- `disagreeingPrice` — a disagreeing price never donates its time.
+- `outOfRangeTime` — an out-of-range time is dropped, never stored.
+- `primitiveTime` — an array, an object or a boolean is never a time.
+- `rereadBackfill` — a time first supplied on an equal-quantity reread is kept.
+- `unknownWindow` — an unreadable fill returns the distinct string `'unknown'`.
+- `unknownBrokerId` — an unreadable fill still records the verified broker id.
+- `timeKeyOrder` — the first readable time key wins.
+- `timeBoundary` — the inclusive boundary and one millisecond past it.
+
+## Two deviations from the source at `7cf4e72a`
+
+The slice is the source's ownership module, with two corrections the self-review
+found. Both are safe for the current callers and both are inherited by the later
+slices.
+
+1. `executionMs` reads only a primitive string or a numeric timestamp. The
+   source passed any value to `Date.parse`, which coerces `['2026-11-03']` into a
+   string and stores a date the broker never sent.
+2. `groupFill` applies the depth tie-break whenever two records agree on price
+   availability, not only when both are priced. The source returned the root's
+   `clientOrderId` for an equally filled, equally unpriced chain, and sell
+   recovery calls `groupFill` for every record including the root.
+
+## Scope
+
+One code file, 10 additions and 4 deletions against the base plus the 107/3
+source slice (117 counted lines). The proof and this receipt are excluded from
+the gate count.
+
+## Left for later
+
+- **The adapter must distinguish `true` from `'unknown'` before this is used.**
+  `observe` now returns the string `'unknown'` for an unreadable fill, which is
+  truthy. `alpaca_paper_execution.js` `observeExternalOrder` returns that value
+  and its callers test truthiness, so an omitted `filled_qty` no longer throws
+  where it did on the base, and a null fill on a fresh record can mark a matching
+  zero-quantity status authoritative. The adapter slice owns that change; this
+  slice must not be used in production before it lands.
+- The paper adapter still reads `row.status === 'pending_replace'` for the
+  replacement hazard. The durable `replacementPending` flag this slice adds is
+  read by the adapter in a later slice.
+- The executor's fill basis is a later piece. This slice proves the ledger
+  underneath it.
+- An out-of-range fill is an identity conflict here, a contract pinned by
+  `scripts/srw639_owned_unpriced_fill_proof.cjs`. The executor treats the same
+  value as unknown and settles nothing from it. Resolving the two views needs a
+  decision on that test, not a code change here.
+- A readable `pending_replace` observation writes the broker's own status into
+  `record.status`. The hazard is separately on `replacementPending`, and
+  `scripts/srw725_executor_proof.cjs` `cancelLifecycleProof` pins
+  `pending_replace` as a nonterminal status the control path must see, so
+  changing the write here would break an existing test. The adapter slice reads
+  the flag instead.
