+# P23b part 4: entry admission, the send recheck, the clamp and the boot wiring
+
+Refs #1415, Refs #1414, Refs #1396, Refs #1404, Refs #1387.
+
+This slice is the last of the P23b cut. It adds the two call sites the design
+names and the boot wiring, and it changes no rule, no scope and no catalogue
+entry. The module from parts 1 to 3 is unchanged.
+
+## What part 4 adds
+
+`bot2/trade_machine.js` grows by 119 lines and loses 3:
+
+- `ruleEvaluation` and `marketEvents` on `createTradeMachine`, both defaulting to
+  the disabled build.
+- `entriesToday()`: the day's entries from the durable rows, by the entry's own
+  time, with a 26-hour numeric window so the calendar read is bounded.
+- `laneRules`: the gate from part 3, bound to the machine's clock, day, lane row
+  and entry count.
+- The frozen snapshot carries the lane keys whose rule is on.
+- The pre-contract check in the refusal chain, after every existing refusal.
+- The clamp after sizing and before the durable intent.
+- The `dte` field on the durable intent, from the picker's own session distance.
+- The final buy-only recheck immediately before `submitOrder`.
+- The day peak, worst and last-loss time, written by the fill path.
+- The unpriced-close unknown, and its resolution only when no close that day is
+  still unpriced.
+
+`bot2_service.js` grows by 17 lines: the flag resolved once at boot, the typo
+reported and left off, and the shipped calendar read once.
+
+## Decisions for part 4
+
+**The recheck is the last admission, not the second-to-last.** It sits after the
+durable intent, after the submit marker, after the committed-revision read and
+after every other admission, immediately before `submitOrder`. A blocked window
+that opens between the chain and the send therefore still refuses, and the
+recheck reads the clock at the last look rather than the chain's. The broker's
+own admission still runs after it; that boundary is P11b's.
+
+**The recheck reads the frozen settings, not the live lane row.** The keys whose
+rule is on travel with the signed snapshot, so a settings edit between the chain
+and the send cannot change what the entry was admitted under. A lane with every
+mode off adds no key, so its frozen snapshot is byte-identical to today's.
+
+**The chain's refusal is the last in the chain, and the gate is not evaluated
+before it.** The rule check runs only when every existing refusal has already
+cleared, so a frozen account, a stale candidate or a full book never pays for a
+ledger scan, and a rule evaluation can never mask the refusal that was already
+there. The row carries the catalogue record only when the rule is the reason the
+entry was refused.
+
+**The clamp runs before the durable intent.** The sized count is narrowed before
+`intent_saved_at_ms` is written, so the intent and the order always carry the
+same count. A clamp that leaves no contract is the catalogue's own `size_zero`
+refusal, which becomes the sizing reason and refuses the entry.
+
+**The entry count is the day's entries, not the day's completed trades.** An
+overnight position closed this morning is not an entry today. A row with no
+readable time makes the whole count unknown, so the cap refuses rather than
+reading a corrupt row as a free slot. A stamp that cannot be a date is
+unreadable too: it passes `Number.isFinite`, so a window that silently dropped
+it would free the slot the row may hold. A zero fill stamp is the epoch, not a
+fill, so the bar time decides the day.
+
+**The day counters exist only when the capability was on at the rollover.** A
+day that started before the capability was switched on keeps them absent, and
+the total-dependent rules read an unknown fact instead of a clean day. Once they
+exist they always track, so switching the capability off and back on inside one
+day cannot lose the day's high and low.
+
+**An unpriced close makes the cooldown unknown, and one priced close does not
+clear it.** A priced close resolves the unknown only when no other close that
+day is still unpriced: one priced breakeven must not speak for a sibling that
+could still turn out to be a loss. The unknown follows the counter and not the
+flag, so a day whose counters exist keeps the unknown across a same-day toggle:
+switching the capability off and back on cannot turn an unpriced close into a
+clean timer.
+
+**An unreadable flag is off, never a boot failure.** Throwing at boot would stop
+the service before saved-only exit recovery starts, so a typo would take the
+exit loop away from a position that is already held. The typo is reported on
+stderr and changes no admission.
+
+**A missing or unreadable calendar is stale, never empty.** The service reads
+the operator's shipped file once and leaves the document null on any error, so
+the event rule refuses on a stale calendar instead of reading a broken file as
+"no event today".
+
+## Proof
+
+`scripts/srw1415_rule_evaluation_proof.cjs` keeps every assertion from parts 1
+to 3 and adds seven sections. Section E now asserts the call sites rather than
+their absence: the machine and the service are the only runtime callers, and the
+proof is the third.
+
+- R: the seven pre-contract rules refuse through the real machine with zero
+  contract reads, zero quote reads and zero submits.
+- S: the send recheck refuses the premium floor and the 0DTE rule after the
+  durable intent, with the submit marker undone on the disk.
+- T: the clamp narrows the durable intent and the order to the same count, and
+  the same lane without the cap sends the wider count.
+- U: an exit is never refused by an entry rule, even with all eleven rules
+  frozen on the row the exit reads.
+- V: the recorded day replays byte-identical to the state the code before this
+  change produced (`sha256=6e866f03…`), and every mode off changes no decision.
+- W: the fill path writes the day peak, worst and loss time; the profit target
+  and the cooldown read them; a same-day toggle keeps the counters; a priced
+  sibling does not clear an unpriced close; an unpriced close that lands while
+  the capability is off still makes the cooldown unknown and survives a toggle;
+  a window that opens during the send refuses; an undatable entry refuses the
+  cap; a zero fill stamp falls back to the bar time; a previous day holds no
+  slot.
+- X: the service resolves the flag at boot, reports an unreadable value and
+  stays off, and reads the shipped calendar once.
+
+The machine cases are hand-built and the case file says so
+(`machine.synthetic: true`): a fake broker, a temporary ledger and a fixed
+clock. The captured-day replay is the separate control, and it is run on its own
+with `node scripts/executor_day_replay.cjs --day 2026-10-21`, which passes with
+its pre-existing scorecard findings reported and not gated.
+
+## Self-review findings and what was fixed
+
+An independent review of the first head found no critical, four major and two
+minor findings. Three were fixed in this slice:
+
+- The pre-contract gate was evaluated before the existing refusals and only its
+  reason was deferred. It now runs only after every existing refusal has
+  cleared, and a proof case counts the calendar provider to show the gate did
+  not run behind a refusal.
+- A fill stamp that cannot be a date (`1e20`) passed `Number.isFinite` and was
+  then dropped by the 26-hour window, which freed a daily slot. The stamp is now
+  validated as a usable moment before the window, and an unreadable one makes
+  the count unknown.
+- The cooldown-toggle proof switched the capability off after the unpriced close
+  had already raised the unknown, so it would have passed on a production
+  assignment that was conditional on the flag. It now switches off before the
+  close.
+- The service-boot proof matched source text. It now lifts the boot block out of
+  the service and runs it against a stubbed file system and the real
+  environment, so a read that succeeds and is then reset to null fails, and a
+  flag block that throws instead of reporting fails.
+
+Two majors are deferred, because the fix is outside this slice's files:
+
+- The day counters follow `state.daily.realized_usd`, the session accumulator
+  the loss limit already acts on. A fill whose broker execution day is earlier
+  still moves that accumulator when it is processed, so the peak can carry
+  another day's dollars. Making the counters a fold over execution-day deltas is
+  the SRW-1399 design and a separate slice; changing it here would change the
+  loss limit, which this ticket must not do.
+- An unpriced close moves the session total down by the position's cost, so the
+  day's worst can be permanently lowered by a fill whose price never arrived. No
+  Bot 2 lane rule reads `dayWorstRealizedUsd` today, and the cooldown already
+  reads the unpriced close as unknown, so the effect is a conservative fact and
+  not a wrong admission. The same execution-day fold fixes it.
+
+## Baseline failures
+
+Six existing proofs fail on this head and on the base commit alike, so they are
+baseline and not this slice: `scripts/srw754_lock_proof.cjs`,
+`scripts/srw753_lane_review_proof.cjs`, `scripts/srw782_exit_ownership_sync_proof.cjs`,
+`scripts/srw1016_independent_stop_proof.cjs`,
+`scripts/srw755_torn_log_lane_scope_proof.cjs` and
+`scripts/srw753_migration_partial_proof.cjs`. Every other Bot 2 proof and the
+two trade-machine tests pass.
+
+## Size
+
+Two code files, 139 changed code lines (`bot2/trade_machine.js` 119 added and 3
+removed, `bot2_service.js` 17 added). The proof script and the evidence are
+proof and fixture, excluded from the code-line count. Cap: 150 code lines, three
+code files.
